understanding of coefficient-related problems in complex analysis. In this study, we introduce a methodology that centers on establishing relationships between the coefficients of functions belonging to a specified analytic class and those of the associated Schwarz functions. In many situations, this framework facilitates the prediction of precise bounds for functionals, thereby simplifying the computational process.\n\nBy utilizing several key lemmas, most notably those formulated by Libera and Złotkiewicz and by Prokhorov and Szynal, along with additional analytical tools and a careful refinement of computations, we succeed in obtaining improved, and in most cases sharp, coefficient estimates.\n\nWe begin by presenting some essential lemmas concerning Schwarz functions. The first of these is a foundational result due to Prokhorov and Szynal.\n\nLemma 1\n\n([3]). Let 𝜔∈𝔹$\\omega \\in \\mathbb{B}$$\\omega \\in \\mathbb{B}$ be of the form (8). Then, for every pair of real numbers α and β satisfying\n\n(𝛼,𝛽)∈{|𝛼| ≤1 2,−1 ≤𝛽 ≤1}∪{1 2 ≤|𝛼| ≤2,4 27(|𝛼|+1)3−(|𝛼|+1) ≤𝛽 ≤1},$$(\\alpha ,\\beta )\\in \\left\\{\\left|\\alpha \\right| \\le {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}},-1 \\le \\beta  \\le 1\\right\\}\\cup \\left\\{{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\le \\left|\\alpha \\right| \\le 2,{\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{27}}{\\left(\\right|\\alpha |+1)}^{3}-\\left(\\right|\\alpha |+1) \\le \\beta  \\le 1\\right\\},$$\n\n$$(\\alpha ,\\beta )\\in \\left\\{\\left|\\alpha \\right| \\le {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}},-1 \\le \\beta  \\le 1\\right\\}\\cup \\left\\{{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\le \\left|\\alpha \\right| \\le 2,{\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{27}}{\\left(\\right|\\alpha |+1)}^{3}-\\left(\\right|\\alpha |+1) \\le \\beta  \\le 1\\right\\},$$\n\nthis implies that the following sharp estimate holds:\n\nFurther investigations explored determinant bounds for functions related to sigmoid-type mappings [13,14], lemniscate domains [15], and petal-shaped regions [1,16]. More recent advancements were achieved through the estimation of second- and third-order Hankel determinants for additional subclasses (see [17,18,19,20,21,22,23]), thus broadening the geometric scope of coefficient estimates in complex function theory.\n\nMotivated by these advances and the structure of the symmetric class 𝒮∗sym(𝜑 𝐻)${\\mathcal{S}}_{\\mathrm{sym}}^{*}\\left({\\phi }_{H}\\right)$${\\mathcal{S}}_{\\mathrm{sym}}^{*}\\left({\\phi }_{H}\\right)$ defined via subordination to the univalent function 𝜑 𝐻${\\phi }_{H}$${\\phi }_{H}$, we aim to determine sharp upper bounds for the initial coefficients |𝑎 2|$|{a}_{2}|$$|{a}_{2}|$, |𝑎 3|$|{a}_{3}|$$|{a}_{3}|$, and |𝑎 4|$|{a}_{4}|$$|{a}_{4}|$, as well as for the Hankel determinants |𝐻 2,1(𝑓)|$|{H}_{2,1}\\left(f\\right)|$$|{H}_{2,1}\\left(f\\right)|$, |𝐻 2,2(𝑓)|$|{H}_{2,2}\\left(f\\right)|$$|{H}_{2,2}\\left(f\\right)|$, and |𝐻 3,1(𝑓)|$|{H}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)|$$|{H}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)|$. Our approach blends classical analytic techniques with modern subordination theory and inequalities associated with Schwarz functions, offering new insights and refined bounds in the geometric theory of analytic functions.\n\n## 6. Conclusions\n\nIn conclusion, we introduced a new subclass of starlike functions with respect to symmetric points within a recently established domain. Sharp bounds for both the coefficients and logarithmic coefficients were derived. Furthermore, employing a novel and concise proof technique, we obtained exact bounds for the second-order Hankel determinants. The best known estimate for the third-order Hankel determinant was also established, accompanied by a conjecture for its sharp bound to stimulate further research. This work opens several avenues for future investigations, including the study of higher-order Hankel determinants, proving the stated conjecture, or exploring analogous function classes within the same domain. We believe these results provide valuable contributions and serve as a foundational reference for subsequent studies related to this domain.\n\n## Author Contributions\n\nConceptualization and initial idea, A.A.L.; research design and methodology development, A.A.L., A.S.T. and J.S.; software implementation and technical development, A.S.T.; data curation and validation, A.A.L. and J.S.; formal analysis and interpretation, A.S.T.; investigation and experimentation, A.A.L., A.S.T. and J.S.; resources acquisition, J.S.; data organization and management, A.S.T.; original draft preparation, A.S.T.; manuscript review and editing, A.A.L. and J.S.; visual representation and illustrations, A.S.T.; supervision and academic guidance, J.S.; project administration, J.S.; funding acquisition, A.A.L. All authors have read and agreed to the published version of the manuscript.\n\n## Funding\n\nThe publication of this research was supported by University of Oradea, Romania.\n\n## Data Availability Statement\n\nNo datasets were generated or analysed during the current study.\n\n## Conflicts of Interest\n\nThe authors declare no conflicts of interest.\n\n## References\n\n  1. Shi, L.; Arif, M.; Abbas, M.; Ihsan, M. Sharp bounds of Hankel determinant for the inverse functions on a subclass of bounded turning functions. Mediterr. J. Math. 2023, 20, 156. [Google Scholar] [CrossRef] [Scilit]\n  2. Tayyah, A.S.; Atshan, W.G. Starlikeness and bi-starlikeness associated with a new Carathéodory function. J. Math. Sci. 2025. [Google Scholar] [CrossRef] [Scilit]\n  3. Prokhorov, D.V.; Szynal, J. Inverse coefficients for (α, β)-convex functions. Ann. Univ. Mariae Curie-Skłodowska Sect. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Sharp Bound of the Third-Order Hankel Determinant for a Symmetric Domain (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/411145199_Sharp_Bound_of_the_Third-Order_Hankel_Determinant_for_a_Symmetric_Domain)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Sym, then the near-sharp bound of the thir